 preparation)"], "excluded_candidates": ["Acute cholecystitis: less likely due to shock without fever or Murphy's sign", "Hepatic rupture: possible in context of alcohol abuse and weight loss but requires more trauma history", "Ovarian torsion/cyst rupture: could explain acute pain but lacks pelvic exam findings", "Alcoholic hepatitis: typically presents with chronic pattern and less dramatic hypotension"], "alarm_features": ["Hypotension with tachycardia (shock)", "Sudden severe right upper quadrant pain", "Recent significant weight loss"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Gynecologic Emergency with Hemodynamic Compromise", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.6766156805564257, "posterior": 0.6623303855344995, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Sudden exacerbation of RUQ pain during exertion in a hypotensive woman raises suspicion for gynecologic causes (e.g., ruptured ectopic pregnancy) but lacks con…"], "evidence_against": ["Ordered β-hCG test suggests suspicion of pregnancy-related causes, but absence of pelvic exam findings (e.g., cervical motion tenderness) reduces the strength …"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.8, "interrupt_relevance": 0.95, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Hepatic / Hepatobiliary Vascular Emergency", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.015, "danger": 0.75, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Absence of jaundice/hepatomegaly provides weak evidence against Budd-Chiari syndrome (LR- 0.82), which is consistent with early presentation but less so for ot…"]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Biliary Obstruction with Acute Complications", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.011735523539919036, "posterior": 0.005249250607204378, "danger": 0.6, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Rapid weight loss and heavy alcohol use weakly increase biliary obstruction risk (LR+ 4.66), but the absence of gallstone history maintains a moderate counterb…"], "evidence_against": ["No gallstone history weakly argues against biliary obstruction (LR- 0.35), though rapid weight loss and alcohol use maintain some residual risk for de novo gal…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.7, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy", "representative_diseases": []}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Acute Pancreatic Inflammation with Hemodynamic Instability", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.009, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["RUQ pain is atypical for acute pancreatitis, which typically presents with epigastric/back pain, making this a moderate_against finding for the branch."]}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Other Acute Abdominal Emergencies", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.013309563201519221, "posterior": 0.005953311965976578, "danger": 0.5, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 3.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.5, "interrupt_relevance": 0.3, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy", "representative_diseases": []}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Ruptured Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemorrhage", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "reopened", "prior": 0.25732327824479395, "posterior": 0.22797323745144032, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Ordered β-hCG testing provides moderate support for ruptured ectopic pregnancy (LR+≈9.0) due to its high specificity for intra-abdominal hemorrhage in reproduc…"], "evidence_against": ["Lack of pelvic exam findings provides moderate evidence against ruptured ectopic pregnancy (LR-≈0.67), though β-hCG pending could still confirm hemorrhage."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.8, "interrupt_relevance": 0.95, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Hemorrhagic Ovarian Cyst Rupture", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 4.485911742318436e-05, "posterior": 7.203752689160755e-07, "danger": 0.7, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Ordered β-hCG testing provides moderate support for ruptured ectopic pregnancy (LR+≈9.0) due to its high specificity for intra-abdominal hemorrhage in reproduc…"], "evidence_against": ["No documented pelvic pathology history weakly argues against hemorrhagic ovarian cyst rupture (LR- 0.35), though spontaneous rupture can occur without prior im…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.85, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B1.3": {"id": "B1.3", "label": "Ovarian Torsion with Hemorrhage", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.434, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Absence of fever provides minor reduction in likelihood for ovarian torsion with hemorrhage (B1.3), as torsion can present without systemic signs."]}}, "frontier": ["B1.2", "B3", "B5", "B1.2", "B1.1"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the ordering of urinary β-hCG in a hypotensive woman with sudden right upper quadrant pain and no pelvic exam findings strongly support ruptured ectopic pregnancy over other gynecologic emergenc…", "summary": "Ordered β-hCG testing provides moderate support for ruptured ectopic pregnancy (LR+≈9.0) due to its high specificity for intra-abdominal hemorrhage in reproductive-age women with shock."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the patient's athletic build and recent rapid weight loss for a fitness show increase the likelihood of ovarian torsion with hemorrhage compared to other gynecologic causes?", "summary": "Athletic build and rapid weight loss weakly support ovarian torsion (LR+≈1.33) but lack definitive risk factors (e.g., pelvic mass) to strongly elevate its probability."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of cervical motion tenderness or pelvic mass on exam in a patient with shock and RUQ pain provide sufficient evidence to rule out ruptured ectopic pregnancy as the cause?", "summary": "Lack of pelvic exam findings provides moderate evidence against ruptured ectopic pregnancy (LR-≈0.67), though β-hCG pending could still confirm hemorrhage."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the lack of fever or bowel symptoms in a patient with acute right upper quadrant pain and shock argue against ovarian torsion with hemorrhage as the primary diagnosis?", "summary": "Absence of fever provides weak evidence against ovarian torsion (LR-≈0.94), as torsion can present without systemic signs in early stages."}, {"t": 4, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the patient's athletic build and recent rapid weight loss for a fitness show increase the likelihood of ovarian torsion with hemorrhage compared to other gynecologic causes?", "summary": "Athletic build and rapid weight loss weakly support ovarian torsion with hemorrhage (B1.3) as a risk factor for torsion in physically active individuals."}, {"t": 4, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of fever in a patient with sudden severe RUQ pain and shock provide sufficient evidence to argue against ovarian torsion with hemorrhage as the primary diagnosis?", "summary": "Absence of fever provides minor reduction in likelihood for ovarian torsion with hemorrhage (B1.3), as torsion can present without systemic signs."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B1", 0.592], ["B1.1", 0.316], ["B1.3", 0.274]]}, {"top": [["B1", 0.677], ["B1.3", 0.419], ["B1.1", 0.257]]}, {"top": [["B1", 0.662], ["B1.3", 0.434], ["B1.1", 0.228]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": true, "reason": "The patient exhibits shock (BP 80/40 mmHg, HR 110 bpm) with severe right upper quadrant pain, requiring immediate stabilization and investigation for life-threatening causes (e.g., intra-abdominal hemorrhage from ruptured ectopic pregnancy or ovarian torsion).", "required_actions": ["Continue IV fluid resuscitation and monitor for response", "Perform FAST ultrasound to detect intra-abdominal bleeding", "Prepare for urgent surgical exploration if imaging confirms hemorrhage"]}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Emergency override is active due to shock and severe RUQ pain, but urgent imaging remains pending. Leading terminal branch (B1.3) has posterior <0.75, and dangerous alternative B1.1 (ruptured ectopic pregnancy) with posterior=0.228 and danger=0.9 remains unresolved. Tree depth is 2/3, requiring further exploration of high-priority branches."}, "turn_budget_used": 4, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.7, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.4343564277077903, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely the diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Ectopic implantation of a blastocyst"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Inflammation of the pancreas"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Obstruction of blood flow through the hepatic vein"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Obstruction of the common bile duct by radio-opaque stones"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Vascular ectasia within the liver"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'ovarian torsion', 'Hemorrhagic ovarian cyst', 'acute pancreatitis', 'hemorrhage', 'Unexplained fevers', 'gas', 'Gallbladder dysfunction', 'fever', 'back pain'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B1", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.57}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B1.3", "label": "Ovarian Torsion with Hemorrhage", "posterior": 0.434, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 4, "disconfirming": 3, "last_disconfirming_digest": "Absence of fever provides minor reduction in likelihood for ovarian torsion with hemorrhage (B1.3), as torsion can pres…"}, "action_intents": [{"t": 3, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "refute"}, {"t": 4, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 4, "intent": "refute"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B1.1"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=67%, source=both]\n\nBiliary Obstruction with Acute Complications vs Ruptured Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemorrhage:\n  Favours first:  clay-colored stools, dark urine, fatigue, jaundice, loss of appetite\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abdominal pain, abdominal tenderness, abnormal astrocyte morphology\n\nBiliary Obstruction with Acute Complications vs Hemorrhagic Ovarian Cyst Rupture:\n  Favours first:  clay-colored stools, dark urine, fatigue, jaundice, loss of appetite\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, abnormal vaginal bleeding, bloating, chilly and clammy skin, dizziness\n\nBiliary Obstruction with Acute Complications vs Ovarian Torsion with Hemorrhage:\n  Favours first:  clay-colored stools, dark urine, fatigue, jaundice, loss of appetite\n  Favours second: abdominal bloating, difficulty emptying bowel, dizziness, fainting, feeling of fullness in the abdomen\n\nRuptured Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemorrhage vs Hemorrhagic Ovarian Cyst Rupture:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abdominal tenderness, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity\n  Favours second: abnormal vaginal bleeding, bloating, chilly and clammy skin, fullness or heaviness in abdomen, lightheadedness\n\nRuptured Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemorrhage vs Ovarian Torsion with Hemorrhage:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abdominal pain, abdominal tenderness, abnormal astrocyte morphology\n  Favours second: abdominal bloating, difficulty emptying bowel, feeling of fullness in the abdomen, frequent urge to urinate, heavy menstrual bleeding\n\nHemorrhagic Ovarian Cyst Rupture vs Ovarian Torsion with Hemorrhage:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormal vaginal bleeding, bloating, chilly and clammy skin, fullness or heaviness in abdomen\n  Favours second: abdominal bloating, difficulty emptying bowel, fainting, feeling of fullness in the abdomen, frequent urge to urinate\n\nRelated to Ruptured Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemorrhage: abdominal ectopic pregnancy, female reproductive system disease, ovarian ectopic pregnancy\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  A 23-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain → arthralgia → Ruptured Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemorrhage\n  A 23-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain → abdominal distention → Ruptured Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemorrhage\n  The pain has been dull and progressive → arthralgia → Ruptured Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemorrhage"}
